A||||5||mshill||Plenum/Valve Cover color?||||1||
Z||000000||mshill||02-04-2000||03:42 PM||||What color paint is the stock plemun and valve covers on the V6?  Is it orange or red?  I need to paint mine, but at Autozone they have GM Red and GM Orange.  Which one is it?<P>BTW,  what's the best way to paint the plenum and valve covers without getting paint in the groves?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||terryk||02-04-2000||06:33 PM||||I thought it was Pontiac Bright Red.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Harry||02-04-2000||09:19 PM||||Pontiac bright red would be correct.<BR>I have tried taping the grooves and also using RTV with sucess either way.<BR>They both clean up easily.||||reg||6
Z||000003||stimpy||02-05-2000||08:13 PM||||Be careful when trying to restore those things yourself, I have seen some nasty looking outcomes. Usualy after not much time pant is cracking and peeling. Not to mention they are kind of a *****  to prep. If you have a little extra cash, you may want to take them to a professional platers shop and have them powdercoated.  I had mine done in a bright yellow to match a future paint job, and it looks very clean, I will post pics soon. Cost me $110, but it is a much smoother finish and will last forever. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||1FST2M6||02-06-2000||01:34 PM||||Take the pieces to the local radiator shop and have them "dipped" that clean mine right out and off. Then send it to the local powder coating shop (this will NOT crack, peel, flake under the rather extreme temps it's subjected to) you tell them the color and your done. only cost me about $60 to do the upper and lower intake pieces. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||stimpy||02-06-2000||01:40 PM||||$60 bucks is a good deal, but sounds right for doing the intakes.  I also had my valve covers done to match, ad I think I paid a premium price, but the results look sharp, so what the heck.||||reg||1
